#: ../../../LFI.rst:3
msgid "Lightweight Fault Isolation (LFI) in LLVM"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:9
msgid "Introduction"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:11
msgid ""
"Lightweight Fault Isolation (LFI) is a compiler-based sandboxing technology "
"for native code. Like WebAssembly and Native Client, LFI isolates sandboxed "
"code in-process (i.e., in the same address space as a host application)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:15
msgid ""
"LFI is designed from the ground up to sandbox existing code, such as C/C++ "
"libraries (including assembly code) and device drivers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:18
msgid "LFI aims for the following goals:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:20
msgid ""
"Compatibility: LFI can be used to sandbox nearly all existing C/C++/assembly "
"libraries unmodified (they just need to be recompiled). Sandboxed libraries "
"work with existing system call interfaces, and are compatible with existing "
"development tools such as profilers, debuggers, and sanitizers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:24
msgid "Performance: LFI aims for minimal overhead vs. unsandboxed code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:25
msgid ""
"Security: The LFI runtime and compiler elements aim to be simple and "
"verifiable when possible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:27
msgid ""
"Usability: LFI aims to make it as easy as possible to retrofit sandboxing, i."
"e., to migrate from unsandboxed to sandboxed libraries with minimal effort."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:30
msgid ""
"When building a program for the LFI target the compiler is designed to "
"ensure that the program will only be able to access memory within a limited "
"region of the virtual address space, starting from where the program is "
"loaded (the current design sets this region to a size of 4GiB of virtual "
"memory). Programs built for the LFI target are restricted to using a subset "
"of the instruction set, designed so that the programs can be soundly "
"confined to their sandbox region. LFI programs must run inside of an "
"\"emulator\" (usually called the LFI runtime), responsible for initializing "
"the sandbox region, loading the program, and servicing system call requests, "
"or other forms of runtime calls."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:40
msgid ""
"LFI uses an architecture-specific sandboxing scheme based on the general "
"technique of Software-Based Fault Isolation (SFI). Initial support for LFI "
"in LLVM is focused on the AArch64 platform, with x86-64 support planned for "
"the future. The initial version of LFI for AArch64 is designed to support "
"the Armv8.1 AArch64 architecture."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:46
msgid ""
"See `https://github.com/lfi-project <https://github.com/lfi-project/>`__ for "
"details about the LFI project and additional software needed to run LFI "
"programs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:51
msgid "Compiler Requirements"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:53
msgid ""
"When building for the ``aarch64_lfi`` target, the compiler must restrict use "
"of the instruction set to a subset of instructions, which are known to be "
"safe from a sandboxing perspective. To do this, we apply a set of simple "
"rewrites at the assembly language level to transform standard native AArch64 "
"assembly into LFI-compatible AArch64 assembly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:59
msgid ""
"These rewrites (also called \"expansions\") are applied at the very end of "
"the LLVM compilation pipeline (during the assembler step). This allows the "
"rewrites to be applied to hand-written assembly, including inline assembly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:64
msgid "Compiler Options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:66
msgid "The LFI target has several configuration options."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:68
msgid "``+lfi-loads``: enable sandboxing for loads (default: true)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:69
msgid "``+lfi-stores``: enable sandboxing for stores (default: true)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:71
msgid ""
"Use ``+nolfi-loads`` to create a \"stores-only\" sandbox that may read, but "
"not write, outside the sandbox region."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:74
msgid ""
"Use ``+nolfi-loads+nolfi-stores`` to create a \"jumps-only\" sandbox that "
"may read/write outside the sandbox region but may not transfer control "
"outside (e.g., may not execute system calls directly). This is primarily "
"useful in combination with some other form of memory sandboxing, such as "
"Intel MPK."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:80
msgid "Reserved Registers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:82
msgid ""
"The LFI target uses a custom ABI that reserves additional registers for the "
"platform. The registers are listed below, along with the security invariant "
"that must be maintained."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:86
msgid "``x27``: always holds the sandbox base address."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:87
msgid "``x28``: always holds an address within the sandbox."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:88
msgid "``sp``: always holds an address within the sandbox."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:89
msgid "``x30``: always holds an address within the sandbox."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:90
msgid "``x26``: scratch register."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:91
msgid ""
"``x25``: points to a thread-local virtual register file for storing runtime "
"context information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:94
msgid "Linker Support"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:96
msgid ""
"In the initial version, LFI only supports static linking, and only supports "
"creating ``static-pie`` binaries. There is nothing that fundamentally "
"precludes support for dynamic linking on the LFI target, but such support "
"would require that the code generated by the linker for PLT entries be "
"slightly modified in order to conform to the LFI architecture subset."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:103
msgid "Assembly Rewrites"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:106
msgid "Terminology"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:108
msgid "In the following assembly rewrites, some shorthand is used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:110
msgid "``xN`` or ``wN``: refers to any general-purpose non-reserved register."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:111
msgid "``{a,b,c}``: matches any of ``a``, ``b``, or ``c``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:112
msgid ""
"``LDSTr``: a load/store instruction that supports register-register "
"addressing modes, with one source/destination register."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:113
msgid "``LDSTx``: a load/store instruction not matched by ``LDSTr``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:116
msgid "Control flow"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:118
msgid ""
"Indirect branches get rewritten to branch through register ``x28``, which "
"must always contain an address within the sandbox. An ``add`` is used to "
"safely update ``x28`` with the destination address. Since ``ret`` uses "
"``x30`` by default, which already must contain an address within the "
"sandbox, it does not require any rewrite."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:125 ../../../LFI.rst:148 ../../../LFI.rst:226
#: ../../../LFI.rst:247 ../../../LFI.rst:278 ../../../LFI.rst:298
#: ../../../LFI.rst:322 ../../../LFI.rst:345 ../../../LFI.rst:366
#: ../../../LFI.rst:385
msgid "Original"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:125 ../../../LFI.rst:148 ../../../LFI.rst:226
#: ../../../LFI.rst:247 ../../../LFI.rst:278 ../../../LFI.rst:298
#: ../../../LFI.rst:322 ../../../LFI.rst:345 ../../../LFI.rst:366
#: ../../../LFI.rst:385
msgid "Rewritten"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:140
msgid "Memory accesses"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:142
msgid ""
"Memory accesses are rewritten to use the ``[x27, wM, uxtw]`` addressing mode "
"if it is available, which is automatically safe. Otherwise, rewrites fall "
"back to using ``x28`` along with an instruction to safely load it with the "
"target address."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:220
msgid "Stack pointer modification"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:222
msgid ""
"When the stack pointer is modified, we write the modified value to a "
"temporary, before moving it back into ``sp`` with a safe ``add``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:241
msgid "Link register modification"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:243
msgid ""
"When the link register is modified, we write the modified value to a "
"temporary, before loading it back into ``x30`` with a safe ``add``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:269
msgid "System instructions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:271
msgid ""
"System calls are rewritten into a sequence that loads the address of the "
"first runtime call entrypoint and jumps to it. The runtime call entrypoint "
"table is stored at the start of the sandbox, so it can be referenced by "
"``x27``. The rewrite also saves and restores the link register, since it is "
"used for branching into the runtime."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:290
msgid "Thread-local storage"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:292
msgid ""
"TLS accesses are rewritten into accesses offset from ``x25``, which is a "
"reserved register that points to a virtual register file, with a location "
"for storing the sandbox's thread pointer. ``TP`` is the offset into that "
"virtual register file where the thread pointer is stored."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:312
msgid "Optimizations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:315
msgid "Basic guard elimination"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:317
msgid ""
"If a register is guarded multiple times in the same basic block without any "
"modifications to it during the intervening instructions, then subsequent "
"guards can be removed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:336
msgid "Address generation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:338
msgid ""
"Addresses to global symbols in position-independent executables are "
"frequently generated via ``adrp`` followed by ``ldr``. Since the address "
"generated by ``adrp`` can be statically guaranteed to be within the sandbox, "
"it is safe to directly target ``x28`` for these sequences. This allows the "
"omission of a guard instruction before the ``ldr``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:355
msgid "Stack guard elimination"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:357 ../../../LFI.rst:380
msgid "**Note**: this optimization has not been implemented."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:359
msgid ""
"If the stack pointer is modified by adding/subtracting a small immediate, "
"and then later used to perform a memory access without any intervening "
"jumps, then the guard on the stack pointer modification can be removed. This "
"is because the load/store is guaranteed to trap if the stack pointer has "
"been moved outside of the sandbox region."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:378
msgid "Guard hoisting"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../LFI.rst:382
msgid "In certain cases, guards may be hoisted outside of loops."
msgstr ""

